{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Dependents review progress:\n- 3289 (live DC harness): PASSED (rev2). rev1 blocking G6 was a mis-specified conflict precondition — the outbound-field-conflict alert fires only on BOTH-sides divergence (local!=baseline AND remote!=baseline, outbound_field_diff.py:354-362); a bare Jira-side edit with local unchanged is Jira-wins inbound with NO alert. Fixed the live scenario to a converged-baseline + both-sides edit, mirroring 3388's test_concurrent_conflict_alerts. rev2 PASS came back unsigned due to flock contention (60s); re-signed via 'rebar sign-review 3289' (no LLM re-run) → certified.\n- 789c (comments): iterating. rev1→rev2 resolved id-capture Scope gaps; rev2 surfaced 3 deeper data-flow findings, all verified+fixed in rev3: (G6) mutation entry must carry local_comment_key (HLC) and binding_store must reach both enactment loops — it already reaches _create_one (dispatch_one.py:154) but NOT _update_one_dispatch_comments (dispatch_apply_phases.py:147, now threaded); (T3) Cloud comment send (acli_cli_ops.add_comment:469) sends --body <str> with NO ADF encode unlike descriptions (acli.py:205), so added --body json.dumps(to_wire(body)); DC str to_wire unchanged; (G1G2) reconciled the two identity stores — existing per-entry jira_comment_id (bug 85a1) authoritative for inbound-origin, new binding-state map authoritative for outbound-origin, never competing. rev3 launched."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785823350147232001,"uuid":"0b32eb1a-e542-4a25-bd31-a97c0dd8df21"}